WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.170
(intro music)

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:02.580
- Welcome back everybody.

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:04.980
When a feel good story
goes viral on social media

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:06.690
I scoop it up and present it here.

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:07.815
This is what I'm liking.

00:00:07.815 --> 00:00:10.530
(upbeat music)

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:12.690
Okay, so I wasn't aware of this

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:15.384
but apparently some people
leave their favorite recipe

00:00:15.384 --> 00:00:18.210
on their tombstone when they pass away.

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:20.070
Did you know this? I didn't know this.

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:22.800
Oh, you did know... Fine, you're that guy.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.840
But the recipes could be for anything.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.270
Their favorite brownie,
fudge, maybe a cookie.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:28.590
And get this, a woman on TikTok

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:30.000
is hunting these recipes down

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.740
and cooking all of them.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:33.510
Her name is Rosie and she's from here

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:35.640
in Southern California, where she watches

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.740
on NBC four, Los Angeles at three.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:39.330
She's on the line right now.

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:40.446
What's up, Rosie?

00:00:40.446 --> 00:00:42.362
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:00:42.362 --> 00:00:43.390
- Hi.

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:44.223
- Hi.

00:00:44.223 --> 00:00:45.360
- Hello.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.160
- So how did you learn about this?

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.450
I've never heard that people

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:50.806
leave recipes on their tombstones.

00:00:50.806 --> 00:00:52.050
- Oh gosh. It's been a journey.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.660
So, I am a digital librarian,

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:56.610
and I was in library science school

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.440
at the University of Maryland.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.660
I had to take a class on social networks

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.810
and basically make a TikTok
from scratch for the class.

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:05.400
And at the same time I was interning

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:06.804
at Congressional Cemetery.

00:01:06.804 --> 00:01:08.317
And so my professor was like,

00:01:08.317 --> 00:01:11.130
"You should make a TikTok
about your internship."

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:12.690
So I started posting about gravestones

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.390
and cemetery life and,
cemetery life, (laughs)

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:16.320
and things like that.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:19.140
And through that process
I was also starting

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:20.100
to cook for the first time.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.070
It was during the pandemic.
I never really cooked before.

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:24.930
And then all in this process together

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:28.530
I first found this gravestone
for Naomi Miller Dawson,

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:29.445
she's in Brooklyn, New York,

00:01:29.445 --> 00:01:31.530
and she has this beautiful gravestone

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.270
with a spritz cookie on it.

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:35.280
And she doesn't just say
that she likes cookies,

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.110
like it has the ingredients listed out.

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:39.570
So I cooked it, I posted it on TikTok

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:42.990
and it exploded in this way
I never would've expected.

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:45.210
- It's super cool and so personal,

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:46.043
like to put on your tombstone.

00:01:46.043 --> 00:01:47.550
And people are known for like, you know,

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:48.383
something they make.

00:01:48.383 --> 00:01:49.590
It's really special.
I'd never heard of it.

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:52.020
So, what's one of your
favorite recipes you found?

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.700
- Oh my gosh. All of them are so good.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:55.260
They're all, they're all desserts

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:56.760
but they're all very
different from each other.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:58.800
Very connected to the
the person buried there.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.680
But my favorite right now is
this Snicker doodle cookie.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.710
I actually made a plate for it right now.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:05.874
They're Annabell's Snicker doodles.

00:02:05.874 --> 00:02:07.320
Annabell Gunderson,

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:09.540
she's buried in Willits, California.

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:12.840
She was a grandmother. She loved Alaska.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:14.700
She went there 30 times in life.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:16.890
She worked as a bookkeeper
in her local school.

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:19.230
And these cookies are so good.

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:20.610
They also make quite a lot,

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:21.970
they're like good for a family gathering.

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:24.720
- Okay. Okay. Well we
have some snicker doodles

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:25.964
cause I didn't wanna be disrespectful.

00:02:25.964 --> 00:02:27.150
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:29.610
So, I'm gonna try one.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:30.443
Here we go.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:33.000
Oh, look at that, they do make a lot.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:33.833
Okay.

00:02:33.833 --> 00:02:36.451
- Yeah, it makes so many,
but they're so good.

00:02:36.451 --> 00:02:37.710
Now let's try it.

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:38.730
- This is amazing.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:40.800
This is actually my
mother's favorite cookie

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:41.820
is a snicker doodle cookie.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.466
So thank you for this recipe.

00:02:43.466 --> 00:02:44.299
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:02:44.299 --> 00:02:45.903
I'm liking your post right now, okay.

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:48.960
And that's not all. Our
season long partner,

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:50.550
Pilot Pen, makers of friction,

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:52.590
the go to erasable pen of
the Kelly Clarkson Show,

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:54.690
want to celebrate those
creating fearlessly

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:56.340
and just having cool
thoughts just like you.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.140
So, they're gonna give
you a thousand dollars.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:02:58.973
How's that sound?

00:02:58.973 --> 00:03:01.011
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:03:01.011 --> 00:03:02.040
(laughing)

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:03.297
All right, thank you so much Rosie.

00:03:03.297 --> 00:03:04.883
And thank you to all our guests today.

00:03:04.883 --> 00:03:08.250
Margaret Cho, Stephanie
Larson, Angel, Don,

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:09.780
and Richard Marks and his sons.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:11.448
Y'all have a great day and
if it's not (indistinct).

00:03:11.448 --> 00:03:12.281
(audience clapping and cheering)

